<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.8.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01393</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.8.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>269ed262-bc2e-4cf0-8b54-3630742ec1c6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_54e1ee56-0684-4f78-9d2e-b434955a8f28" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For an experiment, a scientist designs a can, 20 cm in height, that holds water. A tube is installed at the bottom of the can allowing water to drain out. </para>
          <para>At the beginning of the experiment, the can is full. Every minute after the start of the experiment, \(\frac23\) of the water is drained.</para>
          <para>The height of the water, \(h\), in cm, is a function \(f\) of time \(t\) in minutes since the
            beginning of the experiment, \(h=f(t)\). Which is an expression for \(f(t)\)?</para>
      
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\( f(t)=20+ \frac{1}{3}t\)</para>
            <para>\( f(t)=20(\frac{1}{3})^t\)</para>
            <para>\(f(t)=20(\frac{2}{3})^t\)</para>
            <para>\(f(t)=20+\frac{2}{3}t\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(f(t)=20(\frac13)^t\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cdc0de8f-f7ad-4b24-a42e-b6c7217de1fa" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A bacteria population is 10,000. It triples each day. The bacteria population, \(b\), is a function of
          the number of days, \(d\). Which equation relates \(b\) and \(d\)?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\( b=10000 \cdot 3^d\)</para>
            <para>\(b=10000 \cdot 3d\)</para>
            <para>\(b=10000(\frac13)^d\)</para>
            <para>\(b=10000+3^d\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( b=10000 \cdot 3^d\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0b3534d1-9dcd-4d60-b28a-7553f9e04c3c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The area, \(a\), covered by a city is 20 square miles. The area grows by a factor of 1.1 each year, \(t\),
          since it was 20 square miles. Which equation expresses \(a\) in terms of \(t\)?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(a=20+1.1^t\)</para>
            <para>\(a=20 \cdot 1.1t\)</para>
            <para>\( a=20 \cdot 1.1^t\)</para>
            <para>\(a=20-1.1^t\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( a=20 \cdot 1.1^t\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5778ce9d-a0fd-4fb0-ab3a-3b9bae8a95dc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The graph below models the water draining out of a tube during an experiment represented by the function, \(f(t) = 20(1/3)^t\).
          What does the value \((2, \frac{20}9\)) mean in the context of the problem? (<media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_10_10_01.jpg"/></media>)</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \(\frac{20}{9}\) centimeters less than at the start.</para>
            <para>After \(\frac{20}{9}\) minutes, the height of the water is 2 centimeters.</para>
            <para>After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \(\frac{20}{9}\) centimeters.</para>
            <para>After \(\frac{20}{9}\) minutes, the height of the water is 2 centimeters less than at the start.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \(\frac{20}{9}\) centimeters.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_55059bdc-fd74-48e4-855f-94fcbe04bbe3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A scientist measures the height, \(h\), of a tree each month, and \(m\) is the number of months since the
          scientist first measured the height of the tree. Is the height, \(h\), a function of the month, \(m\)?
       </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes because the scientist measures the height of the tree each month and gets a value,
        so for each value of \(m\), there will be a height \(h\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_720112a8-813a-4511-bc7c-4bf5a1f76b02" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Cary started with 3 goldfish in her tank, and the population doubled every month. Which is an equation that
          represents the population, \(P\), of goldfish after \(m\) months?
       </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\( P=3+2m\)</para>
            <para>\(P=3(2m)\)</para>
            <para>\(P=3 \cdot 2^m\)</para>
            <para>\(P=2 \cdot 3^m\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(P=3 \cdot 2^m\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1c91e30d-674a-4571-92ed-4e12263bbee2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use this equation for problems 7 – 10:
        \(f(t)=50 \cdot 2^t\)</para>
            <para>What is the initial value?</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The initial value is 50. When
        \(t=0\), \(f(t)=50 \cdot 2^0=f(t)=(50)(1)=50\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The initial value is 50. When
        \(t=0\),\(f(t)=50 \cdot 2^0=f(t)=(50)(1)=50\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e40d2def-b7bf-4139-8f97-ed505f105823" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>What is the growth factor?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The initial value is 2. The growth factor refers to the constant multiplier that determines how the
        function grows or decays as the independent variable changes. In this equation, the growth factor is 2.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The initial value is 2. The growth factor refers to the constant multiplier that
        determines how the function grows or decays as the independent variable changes. In this equation, the growth
        factor is 2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_8b18c59d-a4c8-4193-b9a3-f29c1675d9f2" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the independent variable?
       </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\( t\)</para>
            <para>\( f(t)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(t\), The independent variable is the variable that is manipulated or controlled and is represented by
        \(t\) in this equation.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(t\), The independent variable is the variable that is manipulated or controlled and is
        represented by \(t\) in this equation.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0e063d30-b916-44a4-b667-c051bafdbcb0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What is the dependent variable?
       </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\( t \)</para>
            <para>\( f(t)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(f(t)\), The dependent variable is the variable that depends on or is influenced by the independent variable. In this
        equation, the dependent variable is \(f(t)\), which represents the output or value of the function.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>\(f(t)\), The dependent variable is the variable that depends on or is influenced by the independent variable. In
        this equation, the dependent variable is \(f(t)\), which represents the output or value of the function.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cb878148-c8cb-464b-9a7d-cfe8d9def838" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which equation is most appropriate for modeling this data?</para>
            <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
              <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>x</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>1</entry>
                  <entry>2</entry>
                  <entry>3</entry>
                  <entry>4</entry>
                  <entry>5</entry>
                  <entry>6</entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>y</emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>50</entry>
                  <entry>100</entry>
                  <entry>200</entry>
                  <entry>400</entry>
                  <entry>800</entry>
                  <entry>1600</entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y=25 \cdot (2x)^2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=25 \cdot 2^x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=25 \cdot x^2\)</para>
            <para>\(y=50 \cdot 2^{\frac 1x}\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y=25 \cdot 2^x\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y=25 \cdot 2^x\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
